Translating these formal definitions and analytic models into experimental settings requires accommodating finite measurement precision and sensor noise.

8. Empirical Data Classification and Measurement Uncertainty

Uniform Margin Formalization

When evaluating experimental velocity fields, continuous zero-threshold conditions must be extended to compact sets. For a compact subset D_0 \subset D and a positive margin parameter \varepsilon > 0, a vector field \mathbf{u} is (\varepsilon, D_0)-centrivical if:

u_r \le -\varepsilon \quad\text{and}\quad |u_\phi| \ge \varepsilon \quad \text{on } D_0

Parameter \varepsilon represents an operational threshold margin. Any continuous, strictly centrivical vector field operating on a compact domain automatically possesses a positive margin \varepsilon > 0. An isolated zero of u_\phi fails both the strict definition and all margin conditions.

Interval-Based Uncertainty Classifier

In empirical fluid mechanics, velocity component measurements contain noise, yielding closed uncertainty intervals I_r = [u_{r,\text{min}}, u_{r,\text{max}}] and I_\phi = [u_{\phi,\text{min}}, u_{\phi,\text{max}}]. Data classification follows a strict three-valued logic system:

\text{Verdict} = \begin{cases} \mathbf{CENTRIVICAL} & \text{iff } \sup I_r < 0 \quad\text{and}\quad 0 \notin I_\phi \\ \mathbf{NOT\ CENTRIVICAL} & \text{iff } I_r \subset [0, \infty) \quad\text{or}\quad I_\phi = \{0\} \\ \mathbf{UNDETERMINED} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Epistemic and Measure-Theoretic Justification

In continuous velocity component space \mathbb{R}^3, the subspace corresponding to pure radial inflow (u_\phi = 0) represents a manifold of lower dimension—a set of measure zero. When empirical measurement intervals span zero (0 \in I_\phi), the true physical value of u_\phi could be exactly zero or a non-zero value masked by experimental noise.

Failing to resolve swirl above a noise floor does not constitute evidence of zero swirl. Therefore, interval containment 0 \in I_\phi must remain epistemically uncertified (UNDETERMINED). In contrast, certifying a flow as NOT CENTRIVICAL requires either positive proof of non-inward radial drift (I_r \subset [0, \infty)) or a certified exact absence of azimuthal swirl (I_\phi = \{0\}).

