Signed Transverse Angle (\beta)

To quantify vector alignment in the transverse plane, we define the signed transverse angle \beta:

\beta = \operatorname{atan2}(u_\phi, -u_r) \in \left(-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right) \setminus \{0\}

Because u_r < 0, the quantity -u_r > 0 serves as the positive reference axis for the \operatorname{atan2}(y, x) function. This transformation maps the pure inward radial vector -\mathbf{e}_r to the zero-angle direction (\beta = 0) in the local polar velocity plane. The sign of \beta matches the sign of azimuthal velocity (\operatorname{sgn}\beta = \operatorname{sgn}u_\phi). Positive values (\beta > 0) indicate counterclockwise swirl when viewed from the positive end of axis A (+\mathbf{e}_z). Magnitude |\beta| measures the angular deflection relative to pure inward radial drift.

